Lab 13: Managing Task Execution and Dependencies
Objectives

By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

    Implement conditional task execution in Ansible using when clauses
    Control task execution flow based on variables, facts, and registered results
    Use handlers to manage dependent task execution and service restarts
    Build complex automation workflows using block, rescue, and always
    Deploy a small multi-component application with correctly sequenced tasks

Prerequisites

Before starting this lab, students should have:

    Basic understanding of Ansible playbooks and YAML syntax
    Familiarity with Ansible variables and facts
    Experience with basic Ansible modules (copy, service, package, etc.)
    A working Ansible control node with at least one managed node reachable over SSH

Install Ansible on the control node if it is not already installed:

sudo dnf install -y ansible
ansible --version

This lab environment uses a control node with Ansible installed and one or more managed nodes defined in an inventory file. All work is performed in an isolated practice environment owned by the student.
Lab Environment Setup

Create a working directory and a simple inventory file pointing at your managed node(s). Replace node1 with the actual hostname or IP address of your managed node, and adjust ansible_user as needed.

mkdir -p ~/lab13-dependencies
cd ~/lab13-dependencies
cat > inventory << 'EOF'
[web]
node1 ansible_host=127.0.0.1 ansible_connection=local

[web:vars]
ansible_python_interpreter=/usr/bin/python3
EOF

The sample inventory above uses ansible_connection=local so the lab can be completed on a single machine without extra SSH setup. If you have real managed nodes available, replace the inventory entries with their addresses and remove the ansible_connection=local line.

Verify connectivity:

ansible -i inventory web -m ping

Expected result: each host returns "ping": "pong" with a SUCCESS status.
Task 1: Conditional Task Execution with When Clauses
Subtask 1.1: Basic When Clauses

    Create the playbook file:

vim conditional-basics.yml

    Add the following content:

---
- name: Basic Conditional Task Execution
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    install_web_server: true
    deployment_environment: "production"
    server_role: "web"

  tasks:
    - name: Install Apache when web server installation is enabled
      package:
        name: httpd
        state: present
      when: install_web_server | bool

    - name: Start Apache service in production environment
      service:
        name: httpd
        state: started
        enabled: yes
      when:
        - install_web_server | bool
        - deployment_environment == "production"

    - name: Create web content for web servers only
      copy:
        content: |
          <html>
          <head><title>{{ inventory_hostname }}</title></head>
          <body>
            <h1>Welcome to {{ inventory_hostname }}</h1>
            <p>Server Role: {{ server_role }}</p>
            <p>Environment: {{ deployment_environment }}</p>
          </body>
          </html>
        dest: /var/www/html/index.html
      when: server_role == "web"
      notify: restart apache

  handlers:
    - name: restart apache
      service:
        name: httpd
        state: restarted
      when: install_web_server | bool

    Run the playbook with become privileges (package and service management require root):

ansible-playbook -i inventory conditional-basics.yml --become

Expected result: the play completes with ok or changed for all tasks, httpd is installed and running, and /var/www/html/index.html exists on the managed node with the rendered hostname and role.
Subtask 1.2: Advanced Conditional Logic

    Create a second playbook:

vim advanced-conditionals.yml

    Add the following content:

---
- name: Advanced Conditional Task Execution
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    database_servers: []
    backup_enabled: true

  tasks:
    - name: Set server type based on inventory hostname
      set_fact:
        server_type: "{{ 'database' if inventory_hostname in database_servers else 'web' }}"

    - name: Install Apache on web servers
      package:
        name: httpd
        state: present
      when: server_type == "web"

    - name: Create backup directory when backup is enabled
      file:
        path: /opt/backups
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      when: backup_enabled | bool

    - name: Install backup tool based on server type
      package:
        name: "{{ 'mariadb' if server_type == 'database' else 'rsync' }}"
        state: present
      when: backup_enabled | bool

    - name: Display conditional execution results
      debug:
        msg: >
          Server: {{ inventory_hostname }},
          Type: {{ server_type }},
          Backup Enabled: {{ backup_enabled }},
          OS Family: {{ ansible_facts['os_family'] }}

    Run it:

ansible-playbook -i inventory advanced-conditionals.yml --become

Deliverable for Task 1: both playbooks run successfully with no failed tasks, /opt/backups exists on the managed node, and the debug task prints a message showing the resolved server_type for each host.

Expected result: PLAY RECAP shows failed=0 for all hosts, and the debug output confirms Type: web since database_servers is empty in this inventory.
Task 2: Task Dependencies with Handlers and Blocks
Subtask 2.1: Handlers and Task Ordering

Handlers are Ansible's primary mechanism for making one task's execution depend on whether an earlier task reported a change.

    Create the dependencies playbook:

vim task-dependencies.yml

    Add the following content:

---
- name: Task Dependencies and Order Management
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    web_service_port: 8080

  tasks:
    - name: Install required packages
      package:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: present
      loop:
        - python3
        - python3-pip
        - git
      register: package_installation

    - name: Create application user
      user:
        name: webapp
        system: yes
        shell: /sbin/nologin
        home: /opt/webapp
        create_home: yes
      register: user_creation

    - name: Create application configuration
      copy:
        content: |
          # Application Configuration
          PORT={{ web_service_port }}
          HOST=0.0.0.0
        dest: /opt/webapp/config.env
        owner: webapp
        group: webapp
        mode: '0644'
      notify:
        - update application permissions
        - report configuration change

  handlers:
    - name: update application permissions
      file:
        path: /opt/webapp
        owner: webapp
        group: webapp
        recurse: yes

    - name: report configuration change
      debug:
        msg: "Configuration was updated on {{ inventory_hostname }} at {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}"

    Run the playbook:

ansible-playbook -i inventory task-dependencies.yml --become

Expected result: on the first run, the copy task reports changed, which triggers both handlers in the order listed (update application permissions runs before report configuration change). Running the playbook a second time shows the copy task as ok (not changed), and the handlers do not run at all, demonstrating handler dependency on change state.
Subtask 2.2: Task Blocks with Error Handling

    Create a blocks playbook:

vim conditional-blocks.yml

    Add the following content:

---
- name: Conditional Task Blocks with Rescue
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    deploy_application: true
    security_hardening: true

  tasks:
    - name: Web Application Deployment Block
      block:
        - name: Install web server packages
          package:
            name: httpd
            state: present

        - name: Create application directory
          file:
            path: /opt/webapp
            state: directory
            mode: '0755'

        - name: Deploy application start script
          copy:
            content: |
              #!/bin/bash
              echo "Web Application Started"
              echo "Timestamp: $(date)"
            dest: /opt/webapp/start.sh
            mode: '0755'

      when: deploy_application | bool
      rescue:
        - name: Handle deployment failure
          debug:
            msg: "Web application deployment failed, rolling back."

        - name: Remove application directory on failure
          file:
            path: /opt/webapp
            state: absent
      always:
        - name: Log deployment attempt
          lineinfile:
            path: /var/log/deployment.log
            line: "{{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }} - Web deployment attempted on {{ inventory_hostname }}"
            create: yes

    - name: Security Hardening Block
      block:
        - name: Install fail2ban package
          package:
            name: fail2ban
            state: present

        - name: Start fail2ban service
          service:
            name: fail2ban
            state: started
            enabled: yes
      when: security_hardening | bool

    Run it:

ansible-playbook -i inventory conditional-blocks.yml --become

Deliverable for Task 2: task-dependencies.yml shows correct handler firing behavior across two runs, and conditional-blocks.yml completes with /opt/webapp/start.sh present, /var/log/deployment.log containing one timestamped line per run, and fail2ban installed and running.

Expected result: PLAY RECAP reports failed=0 for both playbooks, and cat /var/log/deployment.log shows an appended entry each time the block playbook runs.
Task 3: Practical Implementation Exercise
Subtask 3.1: Complete Application Deployment

This task combines conditionals, blocks, handlers, and templates into a single deployment playbook.

    Prepare the application directory and requirements file. Because /opt/webapp requires root privileges to create, use sudo directly on the control node's shell for this preparatory step (the playbook itself uses become for its own tasks):

sudo mkdir -p /opt/webapp
sudo bash -c 'cat > /opt/webapp/requirements.txt << EOF
Flask==2.3.3
PyMySQL==1.1.0
EOF'

Verify the file was created correctly:

cat /opt/webapp/requirements.txt

Expected output:

Flask==2.3.3
PyMySQL==1.1.0

    Create a templates directory and the required Jinja2 templates:

mkdir -p templates

    Create the application template:

vim templates/application.py.j2

#!/usr/bin/env python3
from flask import Flask, jsonify

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def home():
    return jsonify({
        'application': '{{ app_name }}',
        'version': '{{ app_version }}',
        'environment': '{{ environment }}',
        'status': 'running'
    })

@app.route('/health')
def health():
    return jsonify({'status': 'healthy'}), 200

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)

    Create the systemd service template:

vim templates/app.service.j2

[Unit]
Description={{ app_name }} Application
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User={{ app_name }}
Group={{ app_name }}
WorkingDirectory=/opt/{{ app_name }}
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /opt/{{ app_name }}/app.py
Restart=always
RestartSec=10

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

    Create the deployment report template:

vim templates/deployment_report.txt.j2

Deployment Report
=================

Application Details:
- Name: {{ app_name }}
- Version: {{ app_version }}
- Environment: {{ environment }}
- Deployment Date: {{ ansible_date_time.iso8601 }}
- Target Host: {{ inventory_hostname }}

Components Deployed:
- Base Packages: Installed
- Application Code: Deployed
- Configuration: {{ 'Completed' if enable_monitoring else 'Pending' }}
- Database: {{ 'Configured' if database_required else 'Not Required' }}
- Services: Running

Status: Deployment Successful

    Create the main deployment playbook:

vim complete-deployment.yml

---
- name: Complete Application Deployment with Dependencies
  hosts: all
  gather_facts: yes
  vars:
    app_name: "webapp"
    app_version: "1.0.0"
    environment: "production"
    enable_monitoring: true
    database_required: false

  pre_tasks:
    - name: Validate deployment parameters
      assert:
        that:
          - app_name is defined
          - app_version is defined
          - environment in ['development', 'staging', 'production']
        fail_msg: "Invalid deployment parameters"

    - name: Create deployment log directory
      file:
        path: /var/log/deployment
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'

  tasks:
    - name: System Preparation Phase
      block:
        - name: Install base packages
          package:
            name:
              - python3
              - python3-pip
              - git
            state: present
          register: base_packages

        - name: Create application user
          user:
            name: "{{ app_name }}"
            system: yes
            shell: /sbin/nologin
            home: "/opt/{{ app_name }}"
            create_home: yes

        - name: Create application directories
          file:
            path: "{{ item }}"
            state: directory
            owner: "{{ app_name }}"
            group: "{{ app_name }}"
            mode: '0755'
          loop:
            - "/opt/{{ app_name }}/logs"
            - "/opt/{{ app_name }}/config"

      tags: preparation

    - name: Application Deployment Phase
      block:
        - name: Install Flask python package
          pip:
            name:
              - flask
            state: present
          register: python_deps

        - name: Deploy application code
          template:
            src: application.py.j2
            dest: "/opt/{{ app_name }}/app.py"
            owner: "{{ app_name }}"
            group: "{{ app_name }}"
            mode: '0755'
          when: python_deps is succeeded
          notify: restart application

        - name: Create systemd service
          template:
            src: app.service.j2
            dest: "/etc/systemd/system/{{ app_name }}.service"
            mode: '0644'
          notify:
            - reload systemd
            - restart application

      when: base_packages is succeeded
      tags: application

  post_tasks:
    - name: Check application service status
      service_facts:

    - name: Generate deployment report
      template:
        src: deployment_report.txt.j2
        dest: "/var/log/deployment/{{ app_name }}-{{ ansible_date_time.epoch }}.txt"

    - name: Display deployment summary
      debug:
        msg: |
          Deployment Summary:
          - Application: {{ app_name }} v{{ app_version }}
          - Environment: {{ environment }}
          - Database: {{ 'Configured' if database_required else 'Not Required' }}
          - Monitoring: {{ 'Enabled' if enable_monitoring else 'Disabled' }}

  handlers:
    - name: reload systemd
      systemd:
        daemon_reload: yes

    - name: restart application
      service:
        name: "{{ app_name }}"
        state: restarted
        enabled: yes

    Run the complete deployment playbook:

ansible-playbook -i inventory complete-deployment.yml --become

Deliverable for Task 3: the play completes with failed=0, /opt/webapp/app.py and the webapp systemd service both exist on the managed node, and a deployment report file exists under /var/log/deployment/ containing the completed status block ending in Status: Deployment Successful.

Expected result: run sudo systemctl status webapp on the managed node to confirm the service is active (running), and run cat /var/log/deployment/webapp-*.txt to confirm the report was generated without any unrendered Jinja2 syntax.
Expected Outcomes

    Two conditional playbooks (conditional-basics.yml, advanced-conditionals.yml) that execute tasks selectively based on variables and facts
    A handler-driven playbook (task-dependencies.yml) demonstrating change-based task dependency, plus a block/rescue/always playbook (conditional-blocks.yml)
    A fully working complete-deployment.yml that provisions a Flask application as a systemd service and produces a valid deployment report

Troubleshooting

    Symptom: a task fails with Missing sudo password or a permission denied error when installing packages or writing to /opt or /etc. Diagnostic hint: check whether you passed --become on the ansible-playbook command line and whether the connecting user has passwordless sudo configured on the managed node.
    Symptom: a handler does not appear to run even though you expected a configuration change. Diagnostic hint: handlers only fire when the notifying task reports changed; re-run with -v and inspect whether the notifying task actually shows changed in its result, and confirm the handler name in notify matches the handler's name exactly.

Conclusion

In this lab, you built and ran a series of Ansible playbooks that progressively introduced conditional task execution with when clauses, task ordering and dependency management through handlers, and resilient workflows using block, rescue, and always. You then combined these techniques into a single deployment playbook that installed prerequisites, deployed a Flask application as a systemd service, and generated a deployment report using a Jinja2 template. Along the way you practiced diagnosing common real-world issues such as missing privilege escalation and handlers not firing due to unchanged task state. These patterns of conditional logic, ordered dependencies, and error-handled blocks are foundational to writing production-grade Ansible automation that can be safely and predictably re-run against live infrastructure.
